---
/**
 * Accessibility statement.
 *
 * Every claim on this page was measured against the built site rather than
 * asserted, and the section that says what is still wrong is the point of it.
 * If you change the site in a way that breaks one of these, change the sentence
 * too — a statement that has drifted from the site is worse than none.
 *
 * The measurements: every page carries a language and exactly one first-level
 * heading with no level skipped; every form control resolves to a label; every
 * focusable element shows a focus ring; no text falls below its WCAG AA
 * contrast threshold; every image carries alt text; every page renders in full
 * with scripting off; no page scrolls sideways between 390 and 2560 wide.
 */
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';

const LAST_REVIEWED = '7 September 2026';
---

<Doc
	title="Accessibility — The Itti Foundation"
	description="What The Itti Foundation has done to make this site usable, what has been measured, and what is still wrong with it."
	eyebrow="Accessibility"
	heading="Who this site is built for."
	lede="What we have done, what we have measured, and what is still wrong."
	updated={LAST_REVIEWED}
>
	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">Why we take this seriously</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				One of the three things this Foundation works on is neurodivergence. A site that
				is hard to read, hard to operate without a mouse, or that moves when someone has
				asked it not to, would be arguing against itself.
			</p>
			<p>
				We aim at <strong>WCAG 2.2, Level AA</strong>. We are not claiming a certificate
				against it. What follows is what we have actually tested.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">What has been measured</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				Each of these was checked against the built site, on every page, at widths from
				a phone to a large desktop display.
			</p>
			<dl class="doc__list">
				<dt>Contrast</dt>
				<dd>
					No text on the site falls below its AA threshold &mdash; 4.5 to 1 for normal
					text, 3 to 1 for large. Three of our four accent colours used to fail as small
					type and have been darkened for that use; they are unchanged as rules and
					marks.
				</dd>
				<dt>Keyboard</dt>
				<dd>
					Everything you can click, you can reach with Tab, and every one of those shows
					a visible focus ring. A skip link is the first thing you reach on every page.
				</dd>
				<dt>Structure</dt>
				<dd>
					Every page declares its language, carries one first-level heading, skips no
					heading level, and marks its header, navigation, main content and footer so a
					screen reader can jump between them.
				</dd>
				<dt>Forms</dt>
				<dd>
					All twenty-eight controls on the registration form resolve to a label. Errors
					are announced, not only coloured.
				</dd>
				<dt>Images</dt>
				<dd>
					Every image carries alt text. The artwork in the opening is marked decorative,
					because the words beside it carry the meaning.
				</dd>
				<dt>Without JavaScript</dt>
				<dd>
					Every page renders in full with scripting switched off. Nothing is hidden
					behind a script that has to run first.
				</dd>
				<dt>Motion</dt>
				<dd>
					If your system asks for reduced motion, the opening stops moving: no camera,
					no dissolve, no drift. The three pictures still change, so nothing is lost
					&mdash; they simply cut rather than fade.
				</dd>
				<dt>Zoom and small screens</dt>
				<dd>
					No page scrolls sideways at any width between a 390-pixel phone and a
					2560-pixel display.
				</dd>
			</dl>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">What is still wrong</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				This is the part most statements leave out. These are known, and they are ours
				to fix.
			</p>
			<dl class="doc__list">
				<dt>No one has tested this with a screen reader in anger</dt>
				<dd>
					The structure is right by measurement, which is not the same as being good to
					listen to. We have not sat with anyone who uses one daily.
				</dd>
				<dt>No independent audit</dt>
				<dd>
					Everything on this page was checked by the people who built the site. That is
					the weakest form of testing there is.
				</dd>
				<dt>The opening is a moving picture</dt>
				<dd>
					It is the most demanding thing here, and reduced motion is the answer we offer
					rather than a still alternative.
				</dd>
				<dt>Parts of the navigation lead nowhere</dt>
				<dd>
					Much of the site is unwritten. Those links reach a page that says so, which is
					honest but is still a dead end.
				</dd>
				<dt>English only</dt>
				<dd>
					We ask your preferred language when you register and we cannot yet answer in
					it.
				</dd>
			</dl>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="doc__section">
		<h2 class="doc__h">If something does not work</h2>
		<div class="doc__body">
			<p>
				Tell us and we will fix it. You do not need to know what the problem is called
				or which guideline it falls under &mdash; &ldquo;I could not get past this
				bit&rdquo; is a useful bug report.
			</p>
			<p>
				Write to{' '}
				<a class="doc__link" href="mailto:hello@itti.org.in">hello@itti.org.in</a>. We
				answer within five working days. If something here stops you registering, say so
				and we will take your details however is easiest for you.
			</p>
			<p>
				This page was last checked on {LAST_REVIEWED}. When the site changes, it gets
				checked again.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Doc>
